# GN4 API Foundation: Design and Migration Plan

## Tags

* Assigned: alexm, pjotrp (gemma)
* Keywords: GN4, API, OpenAPI, Rust, MCP, LLM, Design
* Type: Enhancements,
* Status: In Progress



## Description

This document is the design note for the genenetwork4 (gn4) API
foundation: the Rust/axum API server that makes GN data discoverable to
LLMs (and humans) via REST, described by an OpenAPI v3 spec generated
from the code with utoipa and served at /openapi.json.

=> https://www.openapis.org/what-is-openapi OpenAPI specification

The gn4 repo:

=> https://git.genenetwork.org/genenetwork4/

Assignments for the follow-up items live in the issue tracker:

=> https://issues.genenetwork.org GN issue tracker

## Status [2026-10-04]

* Foundation shipped and merged to main (2026-10-04): config.rs
  (layered: defaults -> GN4_CONF -> GN4_SECRETS -> env), response.rs
  (envelope), error.rs (RFC-7807 ApiError), routes/ split per domain,
  /openapi.json via utoipa, two test suites with DB skip-on-unusable,
  cargo fmt canonical. Foundation branch deleted after fast-forward.
* Correlation design note written:
  => gn4-correlation.gmi Correlation endpoint design (stub landed)
* Production gn3 verified reachable for the search proxy pilot:
  https://genenetwork.org/api3/search/?query=alcohol&type=phenotype
  returns live xapian results (100 hits default).

## Decisions

1. No separate "API for AI". One new API serves both humans and LLMs;
   existing gn3 REST APIs are maintained as long as needed, then
   retired.
2. The API is described by an OpenAPI v3 spec (petstore v3.0 is the
   reference example) GENERATED FROM THE CODE with utoipa:
   `#[utoipa::path]` attributes on handlers, `#[derive(ToSchema)]` on
   request/response structs, served at /openapi.json (JSON, not YAML -
   Pjotr prefers JSON).
3. Responses must be descriptive: success carries next actions ("list",
   "drill down"); errors carry suggestions.
4. Rust first, because AI coding agents make fewer subtle mistakes with
   the borrow checker and strict typing. Tests in Python. PyO3
   bindings where necessary. This is an experiment: if it fails we
   revert to Python.
5. The MCP server runs in its own container as the LLM-facing layer
   over the same REST endpoints.

### Spec direction [DECIDED 2026-10-03, Pjotr]

Pjotr (Re: October daily report): "Let's start my way - I think it
moves a bit faster. Later we can bolt specs on and regenerate the API
code."

The spec is CODE-DERIVED (utoipa), not authored. Rationale: a route
change that forgets its attribute is a compile error or an obviously
incomplete diff, not spec drift discovered weeks later; the served
spec always matches the running routes by construction.

This SUPERSEDES the authored-spec-tree approach recorded in commit
e4834ab ("Document split spec layout") and in the 2026-10-02 version
of this note (spec/ directory + vendored jsonref bundler + checked-in
openapi.json). That approach is PARKED, not deleted: if hand-authored
specs become necessary later (e.g. spec-first generation of handlers),
bolt them on then and regenerate from them.

## Foundation structure [as built]

```
genenetwork4/
├── docs/
│   ├── SKILL.md              <- reusable practices, written for AI agents
│   ├── genenetwork_datatypes.md
│   └── mariadb_db-webqtl.md  <- schema dump (112 tables)
├── src/
│   ├── main.rs               <- thin binary: config, bind, serve
│   ├── lib.rs                <- app() router; registry of route modules
│   ├── config.rs             <- layered: defaults -> GN4_CONF -> GN4_SECRETS -> env
│   ├── response.rs           <- the descriptive envelope
│   ├── error.rs              <- RFC-7807 ApiError shared by all routes
│   ├── db.rs                 <- read-only MariaDB queries (SQL_URI)
│   └── routes/               <- one module per gn3 API domain
│       ├── species.rs
│       ├── groups.rs
│       └── correlation.rs    <- STUB: contract first, engine later
├── tests/                    <- Rust in-process router tests
│   └── common/mod.rs         <- db_available(): credential probe; skip if unusable
├── test/python/              <- Python tests over real HTTP (1:1 mirror)
└── guix.scm, rust-package.scm
```

Rules:

- One response envelope everywhere. Success: data + next actions.
  Error: message + suggestions. Never ad-hoc JSON per endpoint.
- Every endpoint ships as a pair: Rust in-process test + Python test
  over real HTTP. Clients of the API are not all Rust.
- OpenAPI derives from code; /openapi.json is served from the
  registered routes at runtime, not checked in.
- The LLM-facing surface is still pinned by tests: tests/openapi.rs
  asserts the served document describes all endpoints.
- Tests that need MariaDB probe the configured SQL_URI credentials
  and skip (with a message) when the database is genuinely unusable -
  the Guix build sandbox has no database, and skip (not failure) is
  the correct result there. Port-open is NOT sufficient: a listening
  server can still reject the credentials.
- Build is reproducible: vendored crates via rust-package.scm
  (guix import crate -f Cargo.lock genenetwork4), cargo-build-system,
  Python REST tests run in the Guix check phase.

## Migration pattern: proxy -> native

gn4 does NOT reimplement gn3 endpoints up front. Each endpoint starts
as a thin Rust handler that proxies gn3's Flask endpoint and re-wraps
the result in the descriptive envelope. Endpoints then go native one at
a time, hottest/most-LLM-used first.

```
client -> gn4 (OpenAPI, descriptive)
             |-- native: implemented in Rust
             `-- proxy:  HTTP -> gn3 blueprint -> rewrap
```

This keeps the LLM-facing contract stable while implementations move,
and avoids a big-bang rewrite. During transition two APIs run, but only
one is being written.

## Endpoint rollout plan [agreed 2026-10-02]

Pjotr (Re: AI API): "We should start with search and/or correlations.
I'll do gemma. Make a plan."

Order of work, structure first, then endpoints proxy -> native:

1. [x] Foundation structure - Alex. The axum router skeleton, one
       module per gn3 API domain, config.rs, response.rs, openapi.json,
       the test-pair discipline. SHIPPED 2026-10-03, merged to main
       2026-10-04 (fd0d45b..924bcf3).
2. search - Alex + Johannes. Proxy phase A now (see below), native
   on the improved xapian index as it lands.
3. correlations - Alex. Lift the proven correlation_rust engine in
   (see gn4-correlation.gmi for phasing); cdylib FFI replaces the old
   subprocess pattern.
4. gemma - Pjotr. Coordinates at the OpenAPI-spec level; the
   envelope + proxy/native pattern is his contract.
5. then: mapping figures, MCP server container, next domains by LLM
   usage (sampledata, metadata, lmdb traits).

## Pilot: search

First endpoint is /api/v2/search:

- Phase A: proxy to gn3 /api/search; exercise OpenAPI + envelope on
  real data immediately. gn3 production verified reachable
  2026-10-03 (see Status).
- Phase B: native on the improved xapian index as it lands.

The NL->xapian model becomes a tool in the MCP layer; the
HS-rats/BXD question-answer corpus becomes the search-quality test set.
Owners for both are tracked in the issue tracker.

## Rust scope and the PyO3 seam

Rust owns the API surface AND the compute. PyO3 is the seam, both
directions:

- cdylib FFI: gn3 calls gn4 compute functions directly. Replaces the
  old subprocess pattern (calling Rust binaries via python's system),
  which was a bad idea in hindsight - agreed.
- embedding: a Rust endpoint can reuse gn3 Python logic while we
  migrate.

If the Rust experiment fails, the PyO3 seam is exactly where the
revert-to-Python swap happens without losing endpoints.

